WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:03.160
STEPHANIE WONG: Business agility
is about consuming services

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:04.300
and applications.

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:07.270
With more and more SaaS and
services being consumed,

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:10.030
you don't have to reinvent
the wheel to be productive.

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:11.800
Google Cloud Private
Service Connect

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:13.870
is the solution to
help you accelerate

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:16.540
your consumption of these
services in the Cloud

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:18.610
securely, simply, and quickly.

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:19.240
Stay tuned.

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:22.628
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:27.080
Services like ERP SaaS
applications, database

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:30.770
as a service, financial data
sets, log analytics, and file

00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:33.140
sharing have increasingly
enabled companies

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:34.130
to stay ahead.

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:36.440
That trend and the
migration to Cloud

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:38.930
will only continue
for enterprises.

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:41.750
But diverse services
means more complexity.

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:44.840
Services all live in different
networks and environments.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:47.990
Application developers want to
simply connect to and consume

00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:50.690
each service, rather
than spend time tinkering

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:54.410
with routing tables, IP
addresses, and NAT rules.

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:56.480
In addition, security
has only become

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:59.810
more fundamental to all
aspects of company operations.

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:02.780
Most companies have
requirements to restrict traffic

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:05.630
to private networks,
not allowing public IPs

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:07.880
in order to reduce
vulnerabilities.

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:09.740
Google Cloud Private
Service Connect

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:12.290
vastly simplifies
service connectivity

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:14.180
so you can consume
services quickly

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:15.530
with minimal management.

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:17.480
It's a fully-managed solution.

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:21.020
It lets you setup private
connectivity to your own,

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:23.900
third-party, and Google
services across networks

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:26.450
that belong to different
groups, teams, projects,

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:27.770
or organizations.

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:30.020
If you're trying to set up
connectivity for a workload

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:32.960
to consume data from
services like a GKE

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:34.970
private master,
Cloud Spanner, Cloud

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:37.760
Storage, or a financial
dataset on a private network,

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:41.600
PSC lets you simply set up
an endpoint from your network

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:43.160
and a target service.

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:45.650
If you want to expose a
service like a log streaming

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:48.350
application to be
discoverable, you simply

00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:50.870
set up a service attachment.

00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:54.140
Private Service Connect enables
service-centric networking

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:56.450
by creating an
abstraction layer on top

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:58.430
of our traditional
network capabilities

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:00.860
to make the network
completely transparent.

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:03.140
It helps you consume
services faster,

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:06.050
achieve a service-driven
environment, secure your data,

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:08.479
and simplify network management.

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:11.390
Private Service
Connect is secure.

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:13.460
Traffic doesn't traverse
a public internet.

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:15.170
Your services
interact like they're

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:17.060
on their own private network.

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:18.680
Simple and performant.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:20.583
It takes care of
network configuration,

00:02:20.583 --> 00:02:22.250
so you don't have to
worry about routing

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:25.460
tables, IP addresses, and
unnecessary coordination

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:26.510
between teams.

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:28.880
You don't need to configure
proxies in your network

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:31.820
and it provides
line-rate performance.

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:32.750
Agile.

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:34.400
It lets you focus
on the service,

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.650
instead of network
management, so dev teams

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:38.630
are empowered to easily
set up connectivity

00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:41.750
on their own, unburdening
network engineering teams.

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:44.300
Both teams can independently
get up and running

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:46.220
and be more productive.

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:48.860
For the demo today, I already
created all the networking

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:51.650
resources, instances,
internal load balancer,

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:53.480
and Cloud Storage
bucket represented

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:55.920
in this diagram for simplicity.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.970
We're going to show how a
client in the consumer VPC

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:01.610
can access a new service
in the producer VPC

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:04.160
through a private Service
Connect endpoint and service

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:05.090
attachment.

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:07.940
When the client sends
traffic from its source IP

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:10.220
to the endpoint, the
Private Service Connect

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:13.250
will send the allowed
traffic from the client IP

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:15.500
to another address in
the producer subnet

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:18.680
where the Private Service
Connect service attachment is,

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:20.840
then to the internal
load balancer forwarding

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:23.900
rule to forward traffic
to the backend servers.

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:27.560
I'll then show the client
also accessing a Google API--

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:28.850
a Cloud Storage bucket--

00:03:28.850 --> 00:03:32.510
through another Private
Service Connect endpoint.

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:35.060
I'll start with the producer
VPC with two subnets.

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:38.630
10.0.2 is the producer subnet
that hosts our web servers,

00:03:38.630 --> 00:03:41.210
then the PSC subnet gives
access from the consumer

00:03:41.210 --> 00:03:44.090
network to producer
services being exposed.

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:45.590
To show you the
routes, I can verify

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:47.840
that there is no connectivity
from the consumer VPC

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:51.590
to the producer VPC, just a
default route to the internet.

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:54.650
I only have firewall rules that
allow traffic from those two

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:56.720
subnets, plus the
health check ranges

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:59.870
to make sure my
service is healthy.

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:04.820
If I head over to Private
Service Connect and then

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:10.860
the Publish Services tab, I can
see the psc-producer service.

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:13.730
It's configured to automatically
accept all projects,

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:18.110
and staging-web is my consumer
project already connected.

00:04:18.110 --> 00:04:20.720
This exposed service
is the load balancer

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:24.290
as a backend with a
private IP of 10.0.2.10,

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:27.050
and in frontends, our web
server instances, which

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:29.780
is the service we're exposing.

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:33.260
Now I'll show my consumer
VPC with a single subnet

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:36.560
and different private IP.

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:38.930
And I also have no
firewall rules setup here

00:04:38.930 --> 00:04:40.310
on the consumer side.

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:42.560
Under Routes, there's
nothing but the subnet route.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:44.360
I deleted the default
route to the internet

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:46.670
to show Private Service
Connect enabling connection

00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:47.930
to Google APIs--

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:50.150
in this case, our
storage bucket.

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:52.400
Under Private
Service Connect, you

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:54.860
can see here there's
two connected endpoints.

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:57.500
The psc-consumer endpoint
targets the web servers

00:04:57.500 --> 00:04:59.540
we have on the producer VPC.

00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:01.610
This endpoint ends in .100.

00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:05.180
The pscga endpoint
targets all Google APIs.

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:07.910
This endpoint ends in .111.

00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:10.170
When the pscga
endpoint was created,

00:05:10.170 --> 00:05:12.080
it also created a
new DNS private zone

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:15.110
with DNS name p.googleapis.com.

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:17.180
It doesn't create any
records, but you can now

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:18.950
use Private Service
Connect to target

00:05:18.950 --> 00:05:21.020
Google APIs using
this domain and you

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:23.220
get automatic DNS resolution.

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:25.490
Next, I'll navigate to
the consumer-client VM

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:27.770
with only an
internal IP and I'll

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:30.590
SSH into it using Cloud Shell.

00:05:30.590 --> 00:05:33.110
To show the client using
Private Service Connect to reach

00:05:33.110 --> 00:05:35.240
the web servers in
the producer VPC,

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:37.160
I'll run a curl command
to the web server

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:40.190
in the producer VPC subnet
using its local endpoint

00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:42.200
ending in .100.

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:45.090
And I can see I can
access the server.

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:47.810
Now let's provide
connectivity to Google APIs

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:49.520
with Private Service Connect.

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:52.730
I can query our storage bucket
using the Private Service

00:05:52.730 --> 00:05:54.770
Connect endpoint-- pscga--

00:05:54.770 --> 00:05:57.230
by doing an nslookup
for storage-

00:05:57.230 --> 00:06:01.490
the PSC endpoint name
.p.googleapis.com.

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:05.450
And it returns the pscga
endpoint IP, 100.111.

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:07.700
I also have my gsutil
client configured

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:09.650
to use this private
endpoint, and now I

00:06:09.650 --> 00:06:11.450
can list the bucket content.

00:06:11.450 --> 00:06:13.460
Just to prove that's the
content, if I navigate

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:16.070
to the consumer-psc-bucket
page in the Console,

00:06:16.070 --> 00:06:17.510
it's the same file.

00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:20.450
Going back to the consumer
VPC's Firewall Rules page,

00:06:20.450 --> 00:06:24.530
you can see the psc-subnet
rule now has a hit count of 1,

00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:27.860
because the session
I just established.

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:29.810
The connectivity
between the client VM,

00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:33.230
and producer backends, and the
storage bucket isn't explicit,

00:06:33.230 --> 00:06:35.480
but it's enabled by
Private Service Connect.

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:37.580
It happens privately
and securely

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:40.860
through Google's
backbone network.

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:42.360
With Private
Service Connect, you

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:45.540
can consume services faster
and stay ahead of the curve

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:47.940
because it's a
fully-managed solution,

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:52.020
meet compliance and keep traffic
secure, simplify your network

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:55.650
and move to the Cloud faster,
leverage a strong and growing

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:57.990
list of third-party
vendors and partners,

00:06:57.990 --> 00:07:00.570
and get performance
and scale to keep up

00:07:00.570 --> 00:07:02.833
with your growth
and the competition.

00:07:02.833 --> 00:07:04.250
Check out
cloud.google.com/private

00:07:04.250 --> 00:07:08.010
service connect to learn more,
and check out the links below

00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:09.720
for code labs and guide.

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:11.760
And remember,
optimizing your network

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:13.200
means freeing up your bandwidth.

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:16.550
[MUSIC PLAYING]

